(memory "mcp/local" :on (asks "user wants the agent to work on their own computer: their local files, git, an app on their desktop, or automating a web browser"))
User's own machine: predefined local servers, names start local- , e.g.
(load-mcp "local-files") , (load-mcp "local-git") . Run on user's machine
through desktop app. Only when user asks for their machine. Fails if desktop
app not open.
- (desktop-connected-p) : t if desktop app connected as of this step, else nil.
- Listed only while desktop app connected. Row ends :local , e.g.
  ("local-git" "…" ("git" …) :unloaded :local) . No :local = runs on server.
- Row ends :also-local = toolkit server has own setup for user machine:
  (load-mcp "acme" :local t) . Without :local t it runs on server. Browser
  automation on user's own browser is this: find browser server with
  search-mcps, load it :local t.
- List after :local or :also-local = flags server takes, e.g. (:quiet) :
  (load-mcp "acme" :local t :quiet t) . Only listed flags; others error.
- Toolkit server with own command: (load-mcp "acme" :local t) runs that
  command, not container.
- Notice says when it changes: "desktop app connected: local servers now
  loadable: …" or "desktop app disconnected: local servers gone: …" .
- Local load asks user as call local/<name> , e.g. local/local-files ,
  unless config allows it: (permission/allow local/local-files) or
  (permission/allow local/*) .
- Load fails with message if desktop app not open or user denied: say so,
  don't retry.

(memory "mcp/oauth" :on (asks "user wants to sign in to, authorize, re-authorize or sign out of a connected service"))
login/logout only for :oauth t servers:
- (login "name") gives :logged-in if authenticated. Else, like load needing
  login, asks user to authorize, ends turn; you are told when they answer.
- User authorized: run (await (load-mcp "name")) , carry on with original
  request; redirect captured for you. Only when they hand back bare code,
  run (mcp-authorize "name" "<code>") before that load. Never ask for code
  before that load failed.
- (logout "name") gives :logged-out. Unloads server if loaded AND deletes
  saved tokens, so next (load-mcp "name") or (login "name") re-authorizes
  from scratch. Use to sign out, or force fresh consent after widening
  :scopes, e.g. on 403 insufficient_scope: (logout "name") then
  (login "name") again.
- Accept only predefined OAuth server name; unknown or non-OAuth errors.
  logout no-op-safe when server not loaded.
